@section 13.3 | Modern Physics and Devices

@q 16:1

The **Higgs boson** gives other particles their mass. Peter Higgs predicted it in 1964, and CERN's **Large Hadron Collider** found it on **4 July 2012** (Nobel 2013 to Higgs and Englert). "God particle" is a media nickname. It is a **boson**, named after **S.N. Bose**.

@q 16:4

@q 16:34-35

@qnote 16:34 | Lasers are used in treating **all four**: cancer (photodynamic therapy, tumour removal), heart (opening blocked arteries), eye (LASIK, retina repair, cataract) and kidney (breaking stones), so with no single answer the source marks it (*).

**LASER** = Light Amplification by **Stimulated** Emission of Radiation. Laser light is **monochromatic, coherent and highly directional**. **Theodore Maiman**'s first laser (1960) used a ruby crystal and gave **red** pulses (694 nm).

@q 16:8-11

**Nanotechnology** works at 1-100 nanometres. **Sumio Iijima** described **carbon nanotubes** (1991); C_{60} **fullerenes** ("buckyballs") were found in 1985. Graphene is a single layer of carbon atoms (2004). A **nano hummingbird** is a tiny US spy drone.

@q 16:12

@q 16:28-30

The most accurate clocks are **optical lattice clocks** using **strontium** or ytterbium atoms (JILA, USA); the second is still defined by **caesium** atomic clocks. **MRI** uses **nuclear magnetic resonance** of hydrogen nuclei in the body's water, with strong magnets and radio waves, and no X-rays.

@section 13.4 | Magnetism, Cells and Everyday Devices

@q 16:20

@src1 16:2
@one
Iron, nickel, and cobalt are ferromagnetic substances and are strongly attracted by a magnet, whereas aluminium is
@ans a paramagnetic substance, attracted only by a very powerful magnet
@end

@q 16:21-22

**Ferromagnetic**: iron, cobalt, nickel (strongly attracted; used in magnets and recording tapes). **Paramagnetic**: aluminium, platinum, oxygen (very weakly attracted). **Diamagnetic**: copper, gold, bismuth, water (weakly repelled). Chromium is antiferromagnetic.

@q 16:6

A **dry (Leclanché) cell** has a **zinc** case as the negative terminal and a **carbon** rod as the positive terminal, with ammonium chloride paste; it gives 1.5 V.

